From: postmarketOS rhodep port
Subject: [PATCH] drm/msm/dsi: ask userspace for a modeset after a lane FIFO
 underflow

Moving the backlight while the compositor repaints makes the DSI controller
run out of bytes mid packet: all four data lanes underflow, the panel is
left decoding a truncated stream, and it stays wrong. The screen fills with
corrupted lines and then a solid colour, and it does not come back.

Measured, with the state left in place and inspected rather than cleared:
the error count freezes the instant the screen goes bad -- 22 to 22 over
eight seconds in one run, 17 to 17 over six in another -- while the
compositor keeps drawing and the framebuffer index keeps advancing. The
link is healthy and the controller has recovered on its own. It is the
panel that is stuck.

Nothing in this driver notices. dsi_err_worker() resets the controller for
DSI_ERR_STATE_MDP_FIFO_UNDERFLOW, which does not help a panel, and there is
no notion anywhere of the panel being desynchronised while the controller
is fine. Neither has the vendor driver, incidentally: downstream recovers
the panel from its ESD check, not from the FIFO path.

What does bring it back is re-running the panel's power on sequence, DSC
PPS included. Changing the refresh rate through kscreen-doctor -- a full
modeset, nothing else -- restores it immediately, with no lock/unlock and
no user interaction. So flag the link bad and let userspace do the modeset,
which is what i915 (intel_connector.c) and cdns-mhdp8546 do after failed
link training, and what the kernel-doc for the property explicitly allows:
"the reason for adding this property is to handle link training failures,
but it is not limited to DP or link training".

Three details worth stating, because each was a decision:

DSI_ERR_STATE_FIFO is not usable as the trigger. dsi_fifo_status() raises
it for any non-zero bit of REG_DSI_FIFO_STATUS, and most of that register
is level rather than error -- DLN*_HS_FIFO_EMPTY reads 1 whenever the link
is idle. A new state bit is added for the four DLN*_HS_FIFO_UNDERFLOW bits,
which is what the hardware actually reports here (fifo=88881010 in the
diagnostic snapshots).

The connector is not refcounted. i915 does drm_connector_get()/put() around
its work, but msm's connector comes from drm_bridge_connector_init(), which
allocates with drmm_kzalloc() and rejects a .destroy hook, so the i915
pattern would set up a put() that has nothing to call. cdns-mhdp8546 keeps
a plain pointer and cancel_work_sync()s on teardown; that is what fits here.

And it will not ask twice for the same link. Sixty underflows in six
seconds has been measured, so without a latch userspace would be asked to
redo a modeset while it is still doing the previous one. The latch clears
in msm_dsi_host_power_on(), the equivalent of i915 clearing its flag in
intel_dp_set_link_params(). A retry interval and a cap sit on top so a
genuinely dead panel settles on one dead screen rather than a modeset loop
costing a quarter second of black each time round.

This is recovery, not prevention. The underflow still happens; the vendor
avoids it by programming a command DMA window that mainline never writes,
which is a separate and so far unsuccessful line of work documented in
docs/display-cmd-dma-window-wip/.

Two things about the retry policy, both of which were wrong in the first
version of this patch and both of which produced a panel that stopped
recovering after the third fault of the boot:

The count is forgotten after a quiet minute. Otherwise DSI_LINK_RETRY_MAX
is not a guard against a modeset loop, it is a limit on how many times the
panel is ever allowed to come back -- two recoveries during boot plus one
under test used it up, and the next real fault was met with "link keeps
failing" and a permanently corrupt screen.

And the latch is taken last, once the request is certain. Taken before the
interval and cap checks, an early return leaves it held by a request that
was never sent; since only msm_dsi_host_power_on() clears it, and that runs
on the modeset the request would have caused, nothing ever clears it again.

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/dsi_host.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/dsi_host.c
@@ -22,7 +22,9 @@
 #include <video/mipi_display.h>
 
 #include <drm/display/drm_dsc_helper.h>
+#include <drm/drm_connector.h>
 #include <drm/drm_of.h>
+#include <drm/drm_probe_helper.h>
 
 #include "dsi.h"
 #include "dsi.xml.h"
@@ -93,6 +95,39 @@
 #define DSI_ERR_STATE_MDP_FIFO_UNDERFLOW	0x0008
 #define DSI_ERR_STATE_INTERLEAVE_OP_CONTENTION	0x0010
 #define DSI_ERR_STATE_PLL_UNLOCKED		0x0020
+#define DSI_ERR_STATE_LANE_FIFO_UNDERFLOW	0x0040
+
+/*
+ * The data lane HS FIFO underflow bits of REG_DSI_FIFO_STATUS.
+ *
+ * DSI_ERR_STATE_FIFO on its own is not usable for this: dsi_fifo_status()
+ * raises it for *any* non-zero bit of that register, and most of the register
+ * is level rather than error -- DLN*_HS_FIFO_EMPTY reads 1 while the link is
+ * idle. These four bits are the real fault, and they are what the panel does
+ * not survive: the controller runs out of bytes mid packet and the panel is
+ * left decoding a truncated stream.
+ */
+#define DSI_FIFO_STATUS_LANE_UNDERFLOW					\
+		(DSI_FIFO_STATUS_DLN0_HS_FIFO_UNDERFLOW |		\
+		 DSI_FIFO_STATUS_DLN1_HS_FIFO_UNDERFLOW |		\
+		 DSI_FIFO_STATUS_DLN2_HS_FIFO_UNDERFLOW |		\
+		 DSI_FIFO_STATUS_DLN3_HS_FIFO_UNDERFLOW)
+
+/*
+ * Do not rebuild the link faster than this, and give up after this many tries,
+ * so that a panel which is genuinely broken settles on one dead screen instead
+ * of a modeset loop. Each modeset costs the panel's power on sequence, which is
+ * a quarter of a second of black.
+ */
+#define DSI_LINK_RETRY_INTERVAL_MS	5000
+#define DSI_LINK_RETRY_MAX		3
+/*
+ * How long the link has to behave before the retry count is forgotten. Without
+ * this the count is a lifetime cap rather than a storm guard: the panel stops
+ * recovering after the third underflow the phone ever sees, which is not what
+ * "keeps failing" is supposed to mean.
+ */
+#define DSI_LINK_QUIET_MS		60000
 
 #define DSI_CLK_CTRL_ENABLE_CLKS	\
 		(DSI_CLK_CTRL_AHBS_HCLK_ON | DSI_CLK_CTRL_AHBM_SCLK_ON | \
@@ -151,6 +186,21 @@
 	struct work_struct err_work;
 	struct workqueue_struct *workqueue;
 
+	/*
+	 * Recovering from a lane FIFO underflow. The controller comes out of
+	 * one healthy and stops reporting errors, but the panel is left
+	 * desynchronised and stays that way -- only its power on sequence, DSC
+	 * PPS included, brings it back. So flag the link bad and let userspace
+	 * do the modeset, the way i915 and cdns-mhdp8546 do after a failed link
+	 * training.
+	 */
+	struct drm_connector *connector;
+	struct work_struct link_status_work;
+	unsigned long link_flags;
+#define DSI_LINK_STATUS_BAD_PENDING	0
+	unsigned long link_retry_last;
+	unsigned int link_retry_count;
+
 	/* DSI 6G TX buffer*/
 	struct drm_gem_object *tx_gem_obj;
 	struct drm_gpuvm *vm;
@@ -1612,6 +1662,85 @@
 	return len;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Modelled on cdns_mhdp_modeset_retry_fn() rather than on i915's version: msm's
+ * connector comes from drm_bridge_connector_init(), which allocates it with
+ * drmm_kzalloc() and forbids a .destroy hook, so it must not be refcounted the
+ * way intel_connector_queue_modeset_retry_work() does. A plain pointer plus
+ * cancel_work_sync() on teardown is the pattern that fits.
+ */
+static void dsi_link_status_worker(struct work_struct *work)
+{
+	struct msm_dsi_host *msm_host =
+		container_of(work, struct msm_dsi_host, link_status_work);
+	struct drm_connector *conn = msm_host->connector;
+
+	if (!conn || !conn->dev)
+		return;
+
+	DRM_DEV_INFO(&msm_host->pdev->dev,
+		     "lane FIFO underflow: flagging [CONNECTOR:%d:%s] bad so userspace re-runs the modeset\n",
+		     conn->base.id, conn->name);
+
+	/* Grab the locks before changing connector property */
+	mutex_lock(&conn->dev->mode_config.mutex);
+	drm_connector_set_link_status_property(conn, DRM_MODE_LINK_STATUS_BAD);
+	mutex_unlock(&conn->dev->mode_config.mutex);
+
+	/*
+	 * Must be sent with no modesetting locks held, which is why the
+	 * property is set and released above first.
+	 */
+	drm_kms_helper_connector_hotplug_event(conn);
+}
+
+static void dsi_queue_link_status_bad(struct msm_dsi_host *msm_host)
+{
+	if (!msm_host->connector || !msm_host->enabled)
+		return;
+
+	/*
+	 * A quiet spell means the previous bout is over and the count starts
+	 * again. This has to come first: without it DSI_LINK_RETRY_MAX stops
+	 * being a guard against a modeset loop and becomes a limit on how many
+	 * times the panel is ever allowed to recover.
+	 */
+	if (msm_host->link_retry_count &&
+	    time_after(jiffies, msm_host->link_retry_last +
+			       msecs_to_jiffies(DSI_LINK_QUIET_MS)))
+		msm_host->link_retry_count = 0;
+
+	if (msm_host->link_retry_count >= DSI_LINK_RETRY_MAX) {
+		DRM_DEV_ERROR_RATELIMITED(&msm_host->pdev->dev,
+					  "link failing repeatedly, not asking again for %u s\n",
+					  DSI_LINK_QUIET_MS / 1000);
+		return;
+	}
+
+	if (msm_host->link_retry_count &&
+	    time_before(jiffies, msm_host->link_retry_last +
+			        msecs_to_jiffies(DSI_LINK_RETRY_INTERVAL_MS)))
+		return;
+
+	/*
+	 * The latch goes last, once the request is certain to be made. Setting
+	 * it before the checks above would leave it held by a request that was
+	 * never sent, and only msm_dsi_host_power_on() clears it -- so a
+	 * modeset that never happens would mean it is never cleared, and the
+	 * panel never recovers again.
+	 *
+	 * One request per link bring-up: sixty underflows in six seconds has
+	 * been measured, and userspace must not be asked to redo the modeset
+	 * while it is still doing the last one.
+	 */
+	if (test_and_set_bit(DSI_LINK_STATUS_BAD_PENDING, &msm_host->link_flags))
+		return;
+
+	msm_host->link_retry_last = jiffies;
+	msm_host->link_retry_count++;
+	schedule_work(&msm_host->link_status_work);
+}
+
 static void dsi_err_worker(struct work_struct *work)
 {
 	struct msm_dsi_host *msm_host =
@@ -1622,6 +1751,13 @@
 	if (status & DSI_ERR_STATE_MDP_FIFO_UNDERFLOW)
 		dsi_sw_reset(msm_host);
 
+	/*
+	 * Deliberately not DSI_ERR_STATE_FIFO, which is raised by idle level
+	 * bits as well; see DSI_FIFO_STATUS_LANE_UNDERFLOW above.
+	 */
+	if (status & DSI_ERR_STATE_LANE_FIFO_UNDERFLOW)
+		dsi_queue_link_status_bad(msm_host);
+
 	/* It is safe to clear here because error irq is disabled. */
 	msm_host->err_work_state = 0;
 
@@ -1684,6 +1820,9 @@
 		if (status & DSI_FIFO_STATUS_CMD_MDP_FIFO_UNDERFLOW)
 			msm_host->err_work_state |=
 					DSI_ERR_STATE_MDP_FIFO_UNDERFLOW;
+		if (status & DSI_FIFO_STATUS_LANE_UNDERFLOW)
+			msm_host->err_work_state |=
+					DSI_ERR_STATE_LANE_FIFO_UNDERFLOW;
 	}
 }
 
@@ -2147,6 +2286,7 @@
 		return -ENOMEM;
 
 	INIT_WORK(&msm_host->err_work, dsi_err_worker);
+	INIT_WORK(&msm_host->link_status_work, dsi_link_status_worker);
 
 	msm_dsi->id = msm_host->id;
 
@@ -2160,6 +2300,7 @@
 
 	DBG("");
 	if (msm_host->workqueue) {
+		cancel_work_sync(&msm_host->link_status_work);
 		destroy_workqueue(msm_host->workqueue);
 		msm_host->workqueue = NULL;
 	}
@@ -2592,6 +2733,8 @@
 	dsi_ctrl_enable(msm_host, phy_shared_timings, phy);
 
 	msm_host->power_on = true;
+	/* The link is up again: allow one more recovery request. */
+	clear_bit(DSI_LINK_STATUS_BAD_PENDING, &msm_host->link_flags);
 	mutex_unlock(&msm_host->dev_mutex);
 
 	return 0;
@@ -2768,3 +2911,9 @@
 
 	return msm_host->dsc;
 }
+
+void msm_dsi_host_set_connector(struct mipi_dsi_host *host,
+				struct drm_connector *connector)
+{
+	to_msm_dsi_host(host)->connector = connector;
+}
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/dsi_manager.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/dsi_manager.c
@@ -485,6 +485,14 @@
 		return PTR_ERR(connector);
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * Remember it: the error worker needs a connector to flag when a lane
+	 * FIFO underflow leaves the panel desynchronised. drm_bridge_connector
+	 * allocates with drmm, so this pointer lives as long as the device and
+	 * must not be refcounted.
+	 */
+	msm_dsi_host_set_connector(msm_dsi->host, connector);
+
 	return 0;
 }
 
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/dsi.h
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/dsi.h
@@ -120,6 +120,8 @@
 int dsi_calc_clk_rate_v2(struct msm_dsi_host *msm_host, bool is_bonded_dsi);
 int dsi_calc_clk_rate_6g(struct msm_dsi_host *msm_host, bool is_bonded_dsi);
 void msm_dsi_host_snapshot(struct msm_disp_state *disp_state, struct mipi_dsi_host *host);
+void msm_dsi_host_set_connector(struct mipi_dsi_host *host,
+				struct drm_connector *connector);
 void msm_dsi_host_test_pattern_en(struct mipi_dsi_host *host);
 struct drm_dsc_config *msm_dsi_host_get_dsc_config(struct mipi_dsi_host *host);
 bool msm_dsi_host_is_wide_bus_enabled(struct mipi_dsi_host *host);
